From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 15:34:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02/22] H2b: per-stream decoded-size limit for hayro-syntax's
 stream filters

A flate stream expands by a factor of a thousand or more, so a small file
could demand gigabytes when one of its streams was decoded (a 50 MiB bomb is
a few dozen kilobytes). hayro had no bound.

`Pdf::new_with_options(data, password, DecodeLimits)` sets
`max_stream_decoded_bytes` for the document (the default is unlimited, so
`Pdf::new` and `new_with_password` behave as before). The flate (flate2 and
the pdf.js-port fallback), LZW, run-length and ASCII base-85 decoders stop
as soon as they have produced more than the limit; ASCII hex output is at
most half its input and is checked after decoding. Such a stream fails with
the new `DecodeFailure::LimitExceeded`, which hayro handles like any stream it
cannot decode, and is counted in `Pdf::decode_stats().streams_over_limit` so
a caller can report it.

The limits and counter live in the document's `XRef` and reach
`Stream::decoded_image` through the stream's context. Xref streams (and
object streams found by the repair scan) are decoded before that `XRef`
exists, so they receive the budget explicitly and are limited too.

Tests (crates/core/tests/h2b_decode_limits.rs): a 50 MiB flate bomb under a
16 MiB limit is dropped and counted, and decodes under the default and
256 MiB; flate (zlib, raw, fallback decoder), LZW, RunLength, ASCIIHex,
ASCII85 and a filter chain decode identically at and above their exact size
and fail one byte below it; xref streams; per-document counting across
threads; and a per-thread peak-allocation check that every amplifying
decoder stops early instead of decoding in full.
